/P 20 0 R
/T (In future versions, we plan to use a Raspberry Pi to provide additional processing power for advanced vision models such as YOLOv11 and potentially integrate LiDAR-based SLAM for improved localization and mapping. We would also like to increase modularity by designing a custom PCB to simplify wiring and sensor integration. Finally, we are interested in developing a tracked drivetrain, which could improve performance on ramps and steps while providing an exciting engineering challenge in both mechanical design and control.)
/E (In future versions, we plan to use a Raspberry Pi to provide additional processing power for advanced vision models such as YOLOv11 and potentially integrate LiDAR-based SLAM for improved localization and mapping. We would also like to increase modularity by designing a custom PCB to simplify wiring and sensor integration. Finally, we are interested in developing a tracked drivetrain, which could improve performance on ramps and steps while providing an exciting engineering challenge in both mechanical design and control.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [43]
>>
endobj
62 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 20 0 R
/T (In our first friendly competition on February the 28th, our robot performed much better than in previous years, achieving a score of between 110-200 with detection alone\( the dispenser system was not attached yet\). In our qualifier run, the robot did slightly worse\( 95-130 points\) due to an unforeseen logic error, and the robot running out of power midway throughout the run. However, in a testing run, the robot was able to detect victims reliably, dispense rescue kits and return to its starting point. We were able to achieve first place in the York regional Robocup Junior competition.)
/E (In our first friendly competition on February the 28th, our robot performed much better than in previous years, achieving a score of between 110-200 with detection alone\( the dispenser system was not attached yet\). In our qualifier run, the robot did slightly worse\( 95-130 points\) due to an unforeseen logic error, and the robot running out of power midway throughout the run. However, in a testing run, the robot was able to detect victims reliably, dispense rescue kits and return to its starting point. We were able to achieve first place in the York regional Robocup Junior competition.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [44]
>>
endobj
63 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 20 0 R
/T (The power system is divided into two branches: a high-current motor rail and a regulated electronics rail. The motor rail powers the drive motors through the Carobot V3 motor shield, while the dispensing stepper motor is supplied via a 5V buck converter. The OpenMV cameras are powered from a dedicated regulated supply to prevent voltage transients from causing camera resets. The regulated logic rail supplies the distance sensors, IMU, and colour sensor. The Arduino GIGA R1 operates on 3.3V logic, so all high-current devices are driven through dedicated driver electronics rather than directly from GPIO pins.)
/E (The power system is divided into two branches: a high-current motor rail and a regulated electronics rail. The motor rail powers the drive motors through the Carobot V3 motor shield, while the dispensing stepper motor is supplied via a 5V buck converter. The OpenMV cameras are powered from a dedicated regulated supply to prevent voltage transients from causing camera resets. The regulated logic rail supplies the distance sensors, IMU, and colour sensor. The Arduino GIGA R1 operates on 3.3V logic, so all high-current devices are driven through dedicated driver electronics rather than directly from GPIO pins.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [45]
>>
endobj
64 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 20 0 R
/T (The robot uses a state machine as its core software architecture, navigating tile by tile through the maze. The maze is mapped using a 2D array of custom tile bitsets, storing wall data, victim records, and exploration states. Breadth-first search calculates the return path to the starting tile. The Arduino GIGA's dual-core processor divides responsibilities: the M7 core handles navigation, PID motor control, mapping, and BFS, while the M4 core continuously monitors the OpenMV cameras for victim detections.)
/E (The robot uses a state machine as its core software architecture, navigating tile by tile through the maze. The maze is mapped using a 2D array of custom tile bitsets, storing wall data, victim records, and exploration states. Breadth-first search calculates the return path to the starting tile. The Arduino GIGA's dual-core processor divides responsibilities: the M7 core handles navigation, PID motor control, mapping, and BFS, while the M4 core continuously monitors the OpenMV cameras for victim detections.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [46]
>>
endobj
